the history of American policing has  been a system that's been at the  forefront of regulating black people's  freedom and after slavery their  citizenship and so it doesn't make much  sense to think about policing as a  problem of a few rotten apples because  the problem reaches all the way to the  root today calls for police reformer  everywhere but does piecemeal police  reform actually work if the past 100  years of investigations into police  brutality are any clue the answer is a  resounding no this is why reforming the  police has never been enough this is  Khalil Gibran Mohammed I am a professor  of history race and public policy at the  Harvard Kennedy School the truth is for  a century or literally 100 years there  have been intense official  investigations into systemic police  brutality to be clear we're only  focusing on investigations into the  police the modern police force in the  United States has been around for closer  to 200 years and that's not even  counting precursors to law enforcement  like slave catchers in the south we're  focusing on past race riots and  resulting investigations which have  proven two things one racism and  brutality is systemic within the police  force and two after repeatedly  diagnosing the illness we fail to treat  it over and over again simply reforming  the police has proven to be a futile  task let's review the historical  evidence we're gonna start in the middle  the civil rights movement 1960s dozens  and dozens and dozens of cities become  ground zero for the fight between black  residents and justice where police  officers were concerned I think the  police acted terribly absolutely  atrocious  I saw wanton beating of people who had  not thrown any bottles or bricks or  anything else were merely walking along  the street beating to their head were  bloody and they were lying on the  pavement  riots broke out in Los Angeles Newark  Detroit New York City Atlanta Chicago  Tampa Cincinnati and that's just naming  a few by 1967 the president at the time  Lyndon Baines Johnson called for a  national commission on civil disorders  the colonel Commission found that across  these various cities police brutality is  pervasive indiscriminate stops in  searches that police systematically  harass youths and they added that the  beat patrolman himself is expected to  participate and to file a minimum number  of stop and frisk or field interrogation  reports for each tour of duty a black  social psychologist named Kenneth Clark  testified before the Commission Clark  was astounded that the commissioners  themselves seemed to have very little  awareness of how so many opportunities  to fix policing had come and gone before  Clark tells them I read the report of  the 1919 riot in Chicago as if I were  reading the report of the Harlem riot of  1913 island-wide of 1901 three of 1965  it is a kind of Alice in Wonderland with  the same moving picture we shown over  and over again the same analysis the  same recommendations and the same in  action and those reports Kirk mentioned  they all did sound the same look at  Chicago in 1919  systemic violence took place in the wake  of the stoning to death of a 17 year old  black boy named Eugene Williams he was  swimming with his friends in Lake  Michigan happened to cross an invisible  color line that color line extended from  the beach into the water and when Eugene  Williams crossed it white beachgoers  stoned him to death police on the scene  refused to help the black people of  color they enforced segregation and  black American demand for justice was  met with white violence a wave of  violence directed towards new black  migrants in their neighborhoods pulling  them off the streetcar shooting at them  on the Stoops of their own homes the  violence finally ended after days and  days of rioting the governor of Illinois  call for a Blue Ribbon Commission the  first one to look at systemic police  violence in the United States of America  the investigation pointed out that  police were part of an ecosystem to  enforce those lines of segregation that  protected white interests criminal  justice officials admit it as white men  in law enforcement that they and their  rank-and-file officers profiled  stereotyped and treated african-american  suspects differently and the  recommendations did not lead to systemic  change in fact they led to more racial  turmoil in his testimony Clark also  mentions the Heartland riots of 1935 in  1943 so let's go further down the rabbit  hole to the Great Depression Harlem it  was a young African teenager who had  gone into a local store where he'd been  accused of shoplifting rumors circulated  leading the crowds to believe he had  been beaten by police or worse even  killed in response the community rioted  they were responding to systemic  discrimination and Prejudice where  police officers were expressing the  racism of white New Yorkers against  black Harlemites what the Harlem riot  found out in its investigation about  6,000 arrests more than 90% of them were  for nonviolent mostly status offenses  disorderly conduct suspicious characters  open containers all the things that were  being policed but in fact represented  people living in a low-income community  having to do the kinds of public vices  that wealthier people or whiter people  in this case do behind closed doors but  the mayor buried the report and it was  only because of a black newspaper the  Amsterdam news that the report and later  became published which is how we know  what was in that report in the first  place  Haarlem write it again in 1943 after  police attacked an african-american  soldier who was defending a black woman  before the event proved to be a boiling  point after unending injustice we have  to remember that the numbers of  lynchings of african-americans in the  nation was growing by the thousands such  that by the late 1940s the number had  topped more than 4000 recorded lynchings  and yet 